#
# prima-materia — the theosophical transmission, taken off the register
#
# Taken from traditions/registry-modern.ttl on the structure-driven entrance
# of SPEC 14, the one that comes without a question. The queue is
# queries/coverage-queue.rq. This registration was chosen out of it for a
# reason that is not thinness: the holding already carries a node that cannot
# be checked without it.
#
# originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl holds a 1916 footnote printed
# inside a Pali Text Society translation of an eighteenth century Sinhala
# meditation manual, in which the translator sets out seven centres of force
# with their radiation counts, their colours, the faculties they confer and a
# warning, and names his source: Mr C. W. Leadbeater's The Inner Life. That
# node ends with a list it calls the diagnostic markers, so that a later
# presentation can be measured against them. Nobody had opened the named
# source. This run opened it and measured them, and the measurement is the
# point of the strand.
#
# What it found, in one line each, with the nodes named:
#
# The counts are Leadbeater's and the footnote reproduces them: four, ten,
# six, twelve and sixteen for the first five centres and nine hundred and
# sixty for the seventh. pmc:TheosophyChakrams.
#
# The faculties are Leadbeater's too, and the footnote is faithful: the throat
# centre for sounds at a distance, the brow centre for clairvoyance and for
# magnifying and contracting objects, the crown centre for unbroken
# consciousness. Each stands in the chapter the footnote names, and each is a
# node of this file. That part of the earlier finding is confirmed and not
# merely repeated.
#
# Two things do not match, and both matter.
#
# The sixth count. Leadbeater's own figure for the brow centre is
# ninety-six, and he attributes the figure two to the Indian books while
# saying that a count of undulations of the same character as the others gives
# forty-eight in each half. The footnote drops the ninety-six and gives the
# centre as two halves. The marker list in the earlier node reads that as a
# radiation count of two and lists it beside the others, which neither the
# footnote nor the named source asserts. Corrected in that file in this run.
#
# The warning. Leadbeater's warning is not about the centres below the navel.
# It is about three other centres, which he says Blavatsky calls the lower
# centres, which are not among the seven used in occult development, and whose
# awakening he calls the greatest of misfortunes. The base of the spine and
# the navel are the first two of his seven, and the arousing of the fire at
# the base of the spine is in his account the means by which the rest are
# vivified. The footnote therefore warns against the first two steps of the
# method it is digesting. Recorded at pmc:TheosophyPericulumKundalini and
# corrected in the earlier file.
#
# The three decisions SPEC 14 requires before the first node:
#
# The contact route stays at pm:contactRouteDocumented, and this transmission
# is the one place in the holding where that is an understatement in the other
# direction: it is itself the documented route for a block of registered
# transmissions downstream of it, and the registration says so. The opened
# chapter quotes the Hathayogapradipika by chapter and verse and names The
# Voice of the Silence, so the receipt is in the text and not inferred; what
# this run does not do with that is said at pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal.
#
# The edition: The Inner Life, first series, in the Theosophical Press
# printing of Chicago 1922, read complete for the two chapters that carry the
# material, with The Chakras read at its second chapter for the counts.
#
# The number of citable places: two chapters of one work and one of a second,
# eighteen places entered.
#

pmt:Theosophy a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Theosophy"@en, "Theosophie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The modern transmission of root races, planes and masters, presented as the recovery of a single ancient doctrine underlying all religions."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered with the note that the pedigree claim is the tradition's own central assertion and is contested, that it belongs in the graph as a claim about origin and not as a fact about transmission, and that much of what circulates today as the doctrine of older traditions entered general circulation here. Taken off pm:corpusNamed on 2026-10-08 at The Inner Life, with The Chakras opened at one chapter. The contact route is unchanged at documented and nothing in this file is offered as an independent witness."@en ;
    skos:note "The registration's second sentence is confirmed in the narrow way a reading can confirm it. The seven force-centres with their radiation counts and colours, as this transmission states them, stand verbatim inside a 1916 Pali Text Society translation of a Sinhala meditation manual, attributed there to Yoga; the manual itself places five raptures in the body and knows nothing of them. The case is held at pmc:WoodwardCakkaInterpolatio in originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl, which this run corrected against the source it names."@en ;
    skos:note "Two works of the registered corpus stand as pm:corpusWork without pm:fromWork and are carried by queries/unopened-works.rq: Isis Unveiled and The Secret Doctrine. Neither was opened. The reason is the scope of the strand and not a property of the works, both of which are reachable as full text."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Blavatsky, Isis Unveiled; Blavatsky, The Secret Doctrine; Leadbeater, The Chakras" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:TheInnerLife, pmw:TheChakras, pmw:IsisUnveiled, pmw:TheSecretDoctrine ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 286-291 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmw:TheInnerLife a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Inner Life"@en, "The Inner Life"@de ;
    skos:note "Theosophical talks at Adyar, published in two series. The first series carries the chapters Force-Centres, pp. 286-297, and The Serpent-Fire, pp. 298-303, which are the two chapters this run read complete. Not named in the registration's principal corpus; declared here because it is the work the 1916 footnote in originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl names as its source, and because the printing read carries the chapter the footnote names. The footnote gives the title without an edition, so which printing its compiler had is not established by this run and is a property of that footnote."@en .

pmw:TheChakras a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Chakras"@en, "The Chakras"@de ;
    skos:note "Named in the registration's principal corpus. Opened at its second chapter, The Force-Centres, for one question only: whether the radiation counts are stable across the author's own works. They are, and the later work adds the author's own comment on the leap from sixteen to ninety-six and from ninety-six to nine hundred and seventy-two. The rest of the work is unread."@en .

pmw:IsisUnveiled a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Isis Unveiled"@en, "Isis Unveiled"@de ;
    skos:note "Unopened. Named in the registration's principal corpus."@en .

pmw:TheSecretDoctrine a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Secret Doctrine"@en, "The Secret Doctrine"@de ;
    skos:note "Unopened. Named in the registration's principal corpus."@en .

#
# I. The seven centres, the fire, and the practice
#

pmc:TheosophyChakrams a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "The seven force-centres, their radiations and their colours"@en, "Die sieben Kraftzentren, ihre Strahlen und ihre Farben"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "chakram"@en, "force-centre"@en ;
    skos:definition "The claim that each of the vehicles carries force-centres, points of connection at which force flows from one vehicle to another, visible in the etheric double as saucer-like depressions on its surface, a quarter of an inch beyond the outline of the denser matter and not inside the body. The centres usually employed in occult development are seven and are placed at the base of the spine, the navel, the spleen, the heart, the throat, the space between the eyebrows and the top of the head. A primary force rushes into each at right angles and radiates out again in straight lines like the spokes of a wheel, and the number of spokes determines the number of petals the centre exhibits: four at the base of the spine, ten at the navel, six at the spleen, twelve at the heart, sixteen at the throat, ninety-six at the brow in two halves of forty-eight, and nine hundred and sixty in the outer circle of the crown, which has besides a subsidiary whirlpool of its own with twelve undulations. Secondary forces sweep round each saucer at right angles to the spokes and weave themselves under and over them, and the crossing of undulations of different sizes produces the appearance the Hindu books call petals. Each centre has a predominant colour, fiery orange-red at the base, a blending of shades of red with much green at the navel, radiant and sun-like at the spleen, glowing gold at the heart, blue with a silvery gleaming at the throat, rose with much yellow in one half and purplish-blue in the other at the brow, and indescribable chromatic effects at the crown."@en ;
    skos:note "The counts are the diagnostic markers that originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl asked to have measured, and they are measured here. Five agree with the 1916 footnote exactly. The sixth does not: the source gives ninety-six as its own figure and attributes two petals to the Indian books, adding that each half is subdivided into forty-eight. The seventh agrees on nine hundred and sixty and the footnote drops the subsidiary twelve."@en ;
    skos:note "The author states the status of the counts himself in the second work, and it is worth keeping because it is unusual in this holding. He calls the leap from sixteen to ninety-six sudden and the variation from ninety-six to nine hundred and seventy-two even more startling, says that the factors determining the number of spokes in a chakra are not yet known, and says that before more can be said hundreds of observations and comparisons must be made, repeated and verified over and over again. A transmission that reports its own figures as unexplained and in need of replication is reporting the state of its evidence and not only its doctrine."@en ;
    skos:note "Three others are named and excluded in the same paragraph, and the exclusion is the part the 1916 footnote lost. The author says Blavatsky speaks of three other centres which she calls the lower centres, that there are schools which use these, and that the dangers connected with them are so serious that their awakening is to be considered the greatest of misfortunes. These three are not among the seven and are not located in the text. See pmc:TheosophyPericulumKundalini."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 286-291 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" ,
        "C. W. Leadbeater, The Chakras, chapter II, The Force-Centres, pp. 12-16 (Theosophical Publishing House monograph reprint)" .

pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Arousing the serpent-fire and sending it through the centres"@en, "Das Schlangenfeuer wecken und durch die Zentren fuehren"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "kundalini"@en, "serpent-fire"@en, "the Fiery Power"@en ;
    skos:definition "The procedure to which the capacities of this file are attributed. The serpent-fire sleeps in the etheric centre at the base of the spine; to bring that first centre into full activity is precisely to awaken it, and the arousing is done by a determined and long-continued effort of the will. Once aroused it is by its force that the other centres are vivified, and the course through which it ought to move is a spiral one, in an order which varies for different types of people. Its function in connection with occult development is to vivify the centres and make them available as gates of connection between the physical and the astral bodies."@en ;
    skos:note "The operative core is withheld in the text and the withholding is stated: the author says he has specially and intentionally refrained from explaining how this arousing is to be done, or mentioning the order in which the force should be passed through the centres, because that should by no means be attempted except at the express suggestion of a Master who will watch over his pupil during the various stages of the experiment. So the work describes what the practice does, names its danger at length, and does not say how it is performed. This holding keeps that shape at generalizations/the-text-and-what-is-withheld.ttl; not added to it in this run, because SPEC 16 requires the counter-search against the holding before a generalization is enlarged and that search was not carried here."@en ;
    skos:note "Where a pm:Reworking node belongs and why it is not written. The receipt is documented in the text itself: the practice is named kundalini in Sanskrit, it is called the Fiery Power and the World's Mother after The Voice of the Silence, and the chapter closes its warning by quoting the Hathayogapradipika iii.107, that it gives liberation to yogis and bondage to fools. pm:Reworking requires pm:reworkedClaim to point at an entered node of the transmission received from, and traditions/hathayoga.ttl carries no node for the serpent-fire; it is also not yet migrated to work identifiers, so touching it would oblige this run to migrate it, which is a strand of its own. queries/work-migration-backlog.rq carries that transmission. The verse number is the one the author gives and was not checked against the vulgate in this run; pmc:HathapradipikaRecensionDispute in traditions/hathayoga.ttl holds why that matters."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapters Force-Centres and The Serpent-Fire, pp. 295-303 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

#
# II. The capacities, one per centre, as the chapter states them
#

pmc:TheosophyInfluentiaeAstrales a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Feeling astral influences without knowing why"@en, "Astrale Einfluesse spueren, ohne zu wissen warum"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity attributed to the second centre. When the centre at the navel comes into activity the man begins in the physical body to be conscious of all kinds of astral influences, vaguely feeling that some of them are friendly and others hostile, or that some places are pleasant and others unpleasant, without in the least knowing why."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 295 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyMemoriaItinerum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Remembering the astral journeys"@en, "Sich der astralen Reisen erinnern"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity attributed to the third centre. When the centre at the spleen is awakened the man is enabled to remember his vague astral journeys, though sometimes only very partially; and the effect of a slight and accidental stimulation of this centre is often to produce a half-remembrance of a blissful sensation of flying through the air."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 296 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophySensusAlienorum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Feeling the joys, sorrows and pains of others"@en, "Freuden, Leiden und Schmerzen anderer spueren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity attributed to the fourth centre. Stimulation of the centre at the heart makes the man instinctively aware of the joys and sorrows of others, and sometimes even causes him to reproduce in himself by sympathy their physical aches and pains."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 296 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyClairaudientia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Hearing voices, music and distant sounds"@en, "Stimmen, Musik und ferne Laute hoeren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity attributed to the fifth centre. The arousing of the centre at the throat enables the man to hear voices, which sometimes make all kinds of suggestions to him, and sometimes music or other less pleasant sounds; and when it is fully working it makes him clairaudient as far as the etheric and astral planes are concerned."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the faculty the 1916 footnote in originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl renders as the power to hear sounds at a distance, and the rendering is faithful to the source."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 296 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyClairvoyantia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Waking visions, and clairvoyance on the full arousing"@en, "Wachvisionen, und Hellsehen beim vollen Erwachen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity attributed to the sixth centre. When the centre between the eyebrows becomes vivified the man begins to see things, to have various sorts of waking visions, sometimes of places and sometimes of people; in its earlier development, when it is only just beginning to be awakened, it often means nothing more than half-seeing landscapes and clouds of colour; and the full arousing of it brings about clairvoyance."@en ;
    skos:note "The same chapter reports a further claim about this centre from another work of the transmission and supplies a mechanism for it: it says that The Voice of the Silence holds that when the serpent-fire reaches the centre between the eyebrows and fully vivifies it, it confers the power of hearing the voice of the Master, which the author glosses as the voice of the ego or higher self, and that the reason is that when the pituitary body is brought into working order it forms a perfect link with the astral vehicle. Not entered as its own capacity because the source for it is another work which this run did not open."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 296 and 302-303 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyMagnificatio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Magnifying and contracting minute objects at will"@en, "Winzige Dinge nach Belieben vergroessern und verkleinern"@de ;
    skos:definition "A second capacity attributed to the sixth centre and distinct from the first. Through it the power of magnification of minute physical objects is exercised: a tiny flexible tube of etheric matter is projected from its centre, resembling a microscopic snake with an eye at the end of it, and the eye at the end can be expanded or contracted, the effect being to change the power of magnification according to the size of the object examined. To examine an atom one develops an organ of vision commensurate in size with the atom."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim carries its own identification with an older one, and the identification is the author's: this, he says, is what is meant in ancient books when mention is made of the capacity to make oneself large or small at will, and the little snake projecting from the centre of the forehead was symbolized upon the head-dress of the Pharaoh of Egypt, who as chief priest of his country was supposed to possess this among many other occult powers. Both halves are ordering acts of a modern writer about older material and are recorded here as his, not as the older material's."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept as its own capacity rather than folded into pmc:TheosophyClairvoyantia because the text treats it as a second way in which the centre is connected with sight, gives it a different organ and a different mechanism, and the 1916 footnote carries it separately as the power of magnifying and contracting objects."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 296-297 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyConscientiaContinua a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Leaving the body in full consciousness, and consciousness unbroken"@en, "Den Koerper bei vollem Bewusstsein verlassen, und ununterbrochenes Bewusstsein"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity attributed to the seventh centre. When it is awakened the man is able, by passing through it, to leave his body in full consciousness and to return to it without the usual break, so that his consciousness is continuous through night and day; and when the fire has been passed through all the centres in a certain order, which varies for different types of people, the consciousness becomes continuous up to the entry into the heaven-world at the end of the life on the astral plane, no difference being made by either the temporary separation from the physical body during sleep or the permanent division at death."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the faculty the 1916 footnote renders as the power of unbroken consciousness on all planes, and the rendering is faithful to the source."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 297 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyArousalYieldsInfluentiae a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Arousing the fire through the navel centre yields the feeling of astral influences"@en, "Das Feuer durch das Nabelzentrum zu fuehren bringt das Spueren astraler Einfluesse hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TheosophyInfluentiaeAstrales ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 295 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyArousalYieldsMemoria a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Arousing the fire through the splenic centre yields the memory of astral journeys"@en, "Das Feuer durch das Milzzentrum zu fuehren bringt die Erinnerung astraler Reisen hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TheosophyMemoriaItinerum ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 296 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyArousalYieldsSensus a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Arousing the fire through the heart centre yields awareness of the states of others"@en, "Das Feuer durch das Herzzentrum zu fuehren bringt das Gewahren fremder Zustaende hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TheosophySensusAlienorum ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 296 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyArousalYieldsClairaudientiam a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Arousing the fire through the throat centre yields clairaudience"@en, "Das Feuer durch das Kehlzentrum zu fuehren bringt Hellhoeren hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TheosophyClairaudientia ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 296 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyArousalYieldsClairvoyantiam a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Arousing the fire through the brow centre yields clairvoyance"@en, "Das Feuer durch das Stirnzentrum zu fuehren bringt Hellsehen hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TheosophyClairvoyantia ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    skos:note "The source states a partial case for this one and it is the device that keeps a failed attempt from counting: especially strong vibrations may at any time galvanize one or other of the centres into temporary activity without arousing the serpent-fire at all, or the fire may be partially roused, and in this way also partial clairvoyance may be produced for the time. See pmc:TheosophyIgnisStratificatus."@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 296-297 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyArousalYieldsMagnificationem a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Arousing the fire through the brow centre yields the magnifying organ"@en, "Das Feuer durch das Stirnzentrum zu fuehren bringt das vergroessernde Organ hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:TheosophyMagnificatio ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
