#
# III. The warning, the prerequisite, and the three devices by which no
# attempt counts as a failure
#

pmc:TheosophyPericulumKundalini a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "The dangers of the serpent-fire, with the injuries named"@en, "Die Gefahren des Schlangenfeuers, mit benannten Schaeden"@de ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    skos:note "The injuries are named and are not left general, which is why the caution is worth a node. Some are purely physical: the uncontrolled movement often produces intense physical pain, and it may readily tear tissues and even destroy physical life. That is called the least of the evils, because it may do permanent injury to vehicles higher than the physical. One very common effect of rousing it prematurely is that it rushes downwards in the body instead of upwards and excites the most undesirable passions to a degree at which it becomes absolutely impossible for the man to resist them; such men are said to become satyrs and monsters of depravity, probably to gain certain supernormal powers which bring them into touch with a lower order of evolution, and to escape from that thraldom may take them more than one incarnation. Even apart from this, premature unfoldment intensifies everything in the nature and reaches the lower qualities more readily than the good, bringing with it a great intensification of intellect together with an abnormal and satanic pride."@en ;
    skos:note "The author also states the ground of his own report, which is the part a later reader has to weigh: he says he is not in any way exaggerating the horror of this thing as a person to whom it was all a matter of hearsay might unwittingly do, that he has himself been consulted by people upon whom this fate has already come, and that he has seen with his own eyes what happened to them. The node carries pm:firstPersonReport for that reason and not pm:textualAttestation. The cases are not identified and no number is given, so nothing here is a protocol."@en ;
    skos:note "The correction this run owed to originations/boran-theosophical-insertion.ttl is here. The warning in the source is about three centres that are not among its seven: the author says Blavatsky speaks of three others which she calls the lower centres, that there are schools which use them, and that their awakening is to be considered the greatest of misfortunes; and he adds that there is a school of black magic which purposely uses the downward rush in order to vivify those lower force-centres, which are never used by the followers of the Good Law. The 1916 footnote turns this into the statement that the Buddhist method does not seek to arouse the centres below the navel, a process attended with great danger. In the source the base of the spine and the navel are the first two of the seven that are used, and the arousing of the fire at the base of the spine is the means by which the others are vivified. The footnote therefore warns against the first two steps of the method it is digesting."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence is in these notes because pm:Cautioning cannot carry one, prima-materia#621. This case is unusual for that finding in that the consequences are many and graded, so a single consequence predicate would not have held it either; commented there in the run of 2026-10-08."@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapters Force-Centres and The Serpent-Fire, pp. 287 and 299-301 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyPraerequisitaArousal a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "Moral development, a strong will and pure thought before the fire is touched"@en, "Sittliche Entwicklung, starker Wille und reines Denken, bevor das Feuer beruehrt wird"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TheosophyPericulumKundalini ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The source states the condition and the consequence in adjacent sentences. It is far better to let the fire remain dormant until the man has made definite moral development, until his will is strong enough to control it and his thoughts pure enough to enable him to face its awakening without injury; and no one should experiment with it without definite instruction from a teacher who thoroughly understands the subject, because the dangers connected with it are very real and terribly serious. The consequences of acting without these are the injuries listed at pmc:TheosophyPericulumKundalini, of which the author says that to experiment with it without understanding it is far more dangerous than it would be for a child to play with nitroglycerine, and that no uninstructed man should ever try to awaken it." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    skos:note "The prior step is the caution node and not a practice, because what the source requires first is not an exercise but a condition of the person and the presence of a teacher. The strength is pm:statedNecessity and not pm:prescribedOrder because the consequence of skipping is quoted and not inferred."@en ;
    skos:note "The requirement has a second half which makes the whole unperformable from the text: the author says he has specially and intentionally refrained from explaining how the arousing is to be done or the order in which the force should be passed through the centres, and that this should by no means be attempted except at the express suggestion of a Master who will watch over his pupil during the stages of the experiment. A reader of this work therefore has the warning, the capacities and the prerequisite, and not the procedure."@en ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter The Serpent-Fire, pp. 299-301 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyIgnisStratificatus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "The fire in seven layers, so that a failed attempt means only one layer reached"@en, "Das Feuer in sieben Schichten, sodass ein Fehlversuch nur eine erreichte Schicht bedeutet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the fire exists in seven layers or seven degrees of force, and that it often happens that a man who exerts his will in the effort to arouse it may succeed in affecting one layer only, so that when he thinks he has done the work he may find it ineffective and may have to do it all over again many times, digging gradually deeper and deeper until not only the surface is stirred but the very heart of the fire is in full activity."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of what it does and not because of what it describes. It is the explanation the transmission supplies in advance for an attempt that produced nothing, and it is stated in the source as a general case rather than as a diagnosis of a particular failure. Under it no number of attempts without result can count against the practice, since each is accounted for as a layer reached, and the text says so in the very words a practitioner would use to report a failure, that he thinks he has done the work and finds it ineffective."@en ;
    skos:note "The same page supplies a second account of a result that arrives without the practice: especially strong vibrations may at any time galvanize one or other of the centres into temporary activity without arousing the serpent-fire at all, or the fire may be partially roused, and in that way partial clairvoyance may be produced for the time. So the source covers both a nothing and a something without the procedure having worked."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the shape the holding keeps at generalizations/graded-failure.ttl and generalizations/no-failed-run-counts.ttl. Not added to either in this run, because SPEC 16 requires the counter-search against the holding before a generalization is enlarged and that search was not carried here. A later run has a clean candidate and this node is the witness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, p. 297 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyDilatioIncarnationum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "The attainment deferred to a later incarnation and a later round"@en, "Die Erreichung auf eine spaetere Inkarnation und eine spaetere Runde verschoben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the development of the higher force-centres will come to all in due course, but that most people cannot gain it during the present incarnation if it is the first in which they have begun to take these matters seriously in hand; that some might succeed, whose bodies are by heredity more adaptable than most others; and that it is really for the majority the work of a later round altogether. The conquest of the serpent-fire has besides to be repeated in each incarnation, since the vehicles are new each time, though after it has once been thoroughly achieved the repetitions are said to be an easy matter."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered for the same reason as the node above it. The term on which the capacities of this file are promised is placed beyond any life in which a witness could check it, and the text says so plainly rather than leaving it to be inferred. Together with the seven layers this is the second of two devices in the same two chapters by which no attempt and no lifetime can count as a failure, and the holding keeps that shape at generalizations/the-count-that-does-not-fall-due.ttl and generalizations/counted-thresholds.ttl. Not added to either in this run and for the same reason."@en ;
    skos:note "The hereditary clause is recorded as stated and not reworded. The source says that some Indians might succeed in doing so, as their bodies are by heredity more adaptable than most others. It is an assertion about bodies and descent made by a modern writer and it belongs at this node because it is part of the same sentence that defers the attainment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter The Serpent-Fire, p. 303 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyNullaExceptio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "The objection raised in advance, and answered against the practitioner"@en, "Der vorweg erhobene Einwand, und die Antwort gegen den Uebenden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The passage in which the transmission raises the practitioner's own expectation and disposes of it. The author says that in matters such as these students so often seem to think that some special exception to the laws of nature will be made in their case, that some special intervention of providence will save them from the consequences of their folly; that assuredly nothing of that sort will happen, and that the man who wantonly provokes an explosion is quite likely to become its first victim; that it would save much trouble and disappointment if students could be induced to understand that in all matters connected with occultism we mean just exactly and literally what we say, and that it is applicable in every case without exception, for there is no such thing as favouritism in the working of the great laws of the universe."@en ;
    skos:note "Kept as its own node because it is the place where the failure is assigned. The transmission anticipates the report of a bad outcome and locates its cause in the person before any case arises: everybody wants to try all possible experiments, everybody is convinced that he is quite ready for the highest possible teaching, and no one is willing to work patiently at the improvement of character. The holding keeps this shape at generalizations/displaced-penalties.ttl and generalizations/the-observation-answered-by-narrowing.ttl; not added to either in this run and for the reason given at the two nodes above."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter The Serpent-Fire, pp. 301-302 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophyPetalaNonMoralia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheInnerLife ;
    rdfs:label "The counter-instance the author reports against his own tradition's moral reading"@en, "Der Gegenfall, den der Autor gegen die sittliche Lesart seiner eigenen Ueberlieferung berichtet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author reports having heard it suggested that each of the different petals of the force-centres represents a moral quality and that the development of that quality brings the centre into activity. He says he has not yet met with any facts which confirm this, nor is he able to see exactly how it can be, because the appearance is produced by definite and easily recognizable forces and the petals in a centre are either active or not active according as those forces have or have not been aroused; that their development seems to him to have no more connection with morality than has the development of the biceps; and that he has certainly met with persons in whom some of the centres were in full activity though the moral development was by no means excellent."@en ;
    skos:note "The rarest kind of place in this holding and the reason the chapter was read to its end. A writer of a transmission that states a prerequisite of moral development for the same practice reports, in the same chapter, cases in which the practice had worked and the moral condition was absent, and declines a reading that would have made the two inseparable. The two statements are not formally in conflict, because the prerequisite is about safety and this is about the mechanism of the appearance; but they pull in opposite directions, and the holding has few sources that record their own counter-instances at all."@en ;
    skos:note "Not entered as a pm:Disputing node. The suggestion the author rejects is unattributed in the source, so only one side has a place and a work, and SPEC requires at least two sources for a dispute. A later run that finds the suggestion in a named theosophical work has the second side and can convert this node."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theosophy ;
    dcterms:source "C. W. Leadbeater, The Inner Life, first series, chapter Force-Centres, pp. 291-292 (Chicago: The Theosophical Press, 1922 printing)" .

pmc:TheosophySituatAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "The arousing is placed on the practitioner's own agency"@en, "Das Wecken liegt in der eigenen Handlungstraegerschaft"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:TheosophyKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:axisValue pm:ownAgency ;
    pm:situationGround "The source says the arousing is done by a determined and long-continued effort of the will, that the will should be set in motion to arrest the fire where it rises of itself, and that the occultist is trained to guide it into a spiral course. Where it rises without that effort the text treats the event as accidental or spontaneous and not as given. The coordinate is the compiler's; the source names no axis." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The placement is worth making because the same two chapters make the result depend on a Master who must suggest the attempt and watch over it, which would be a reason to read the agency as ceded. It is not: what the Master supplies is the instruction and the supervision, while the act is the pupil's own effort of will, and the text assigns the consequences of failure to the pupil at pmc:TheosophyNullaExceptio."@en .

pmc:TheosophySituatAcquisitio a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "The capacities are placed on acquisition by effort"@en, "Die Vermoegen liegen beim Erwerb durch Anstrengung"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:TheosophyConscientiaContinua ;
    pm:axisValue pm:byEffort ;
    pm:situationGround "The source attributes the unbroken consciousness to the passing of the fire through all the centres in a certain order, the fire having been aroused by a determined and long-continued effort of the will, and says that the conquest has to be repeated in each incarnation. The coordinate is the compiler's." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The same chapter records glimpses of the astral world that arrive without the fire being aroused at all, through especially strong vibrations that galvanize a centre into temporary activity. Those are not placed here and would take the other value; they are a different claim and are held at pmc:TheosophyIgnisStratificatus."@en .
